# Documentation workflow (intake → pre-coding/LLD → wrap-up + UT evidence)

Three chronological touchpoints in EVERY piece of work — skip none. **Be proactive at all three; do not wait to be asked.**

1. **Intake** (work starts) — confirm the ticket number, transcribe any acceptance-criteria screenshot to plain text.
2. **Pre-coding analysis** (before the first code edit) — map the cascading impact, then author the **Low-Level Design (LLD)** FIRST, then spawn the preliminary `changes/<slug>.md` that summarizes + links to it.
3. **Wrap-up** (work ends) — finalize the `changes/` doc and commit code first, doc second.

All three are part of Definition of Done. Retrieve before any edit ([`retrieve-before-edit.mdc`](./retrieve-before-edit.mdc)); analysis on stale local copies gives wrong answers.

---

## Touchpoint 1 — Intake (run BEFORE any code)

Trigger the FIRST time the user attaches a requirements screenshot (Jira/ADO panel, email, Figma, Slack, slide), says "start/build/fix <ticket>", or mentions a new ticket/case number. When unsure, treat it as a fresh intake. Run all five sub-steps; do not collapse into one message:

1. **Get the ticket number — extract it, or PROACTIVELY ASK for it.** First try to pull the bug/story/case number from what the user shared — the message text AND any attached screenshot/panel. If you can't find one there and the user didn't state it, **proactively ASK before doing anything else** (_"What's the bug/story number for this?"_) — never start work without it. Then read it back to confirm: _"Confirming this is for **<TRACKER-NNN>** — <one-line summary>. Right?"_ Accept Jira (`PROJ-1234`), ADO (`Bug 567890`), Salesforce Case Id, or an ad-hoc reference ("Slack request from John, 2026-04-10"). Do NOT proceed without a confirmed reference; if the user contradicts your read-back, STOP and re-ask — never silently move on with a guessed number.
2. **Name the chat after the ticket.** Once confirmed, rename the current conversation to `<Bug/Story number> - <short description/context from what was shared>` (e.g. `Bug 567890 - PDA activation skips vendor effective date`). In Cursor, use the rename-chat capability (the `cursor-app-control` `rename_chat` tool); in other tools set the conversation title equivalently. If there's genuinely no ticket (rare ad-hoc work), name it `<short description>`.
3. **Transcribe any visual requirement to TEXT.** Screenshots rot and don't survive PR tooling. Read every AC off the image, write them as numbered plain text (`AC1`, `AC2`, …), post them back for confirmation, and hold them through the work. Transcription is mandatory even if the screenshot "looks obvious".
4. **Capture explicit out-of-scope items.** Transcribe any "not now / follow-up / won't fix" items. If none were stated, ASK: _"Anything to mark explicitly out-of-scope?"_
5. **Stash the intake bundle** (confirmed ticket + transcribed ACs + out-of-scope) for wrap-up. It feeds the doc header, §3 In/Out-of-scope, and §7 AC verification.

The §3 ACs in the final doc MUST be the transcribed text — **never** "see attached screenshot".

---

## Touchpoint 2 — Pre-coding analysis + LLD (after intake, BEFORE any code edit)

Salesforce is a heavily-cascading runtime: one insert can fire a process builder → flow → trigger → another trigger updating a third sObject. A "just create one record" change can ripple into 4-5 sObjects and queued jobs. Walk that landscape BEFORE coding, while the accidental ripples are still cheap to design around.

Run these as a `TodoWrite` plan (one entry each) for resumability:

- **E1 — Identify the touched surface.** List every component the change reads/writes/triggers: sObjects (confirm shapes in `config/schema/`), triggers (`grep -l "<sObject>" force-app/main/default/triggers/*.trigger`), flows, validation rules (`ls .../objects/<sObj>/validationRules/`), OmniStudio (`grep` the IP/DR/OS dirs), Apex callers, custom metadata/settings.
- **E2 — Map cascading impact.** For each create/update/delete, walk downstream: which triggers + flows fire, what DML they do, what other sObjects they touch — recurse until the chain terminates. Sketch it (a mermaid `flowchart` for the doc).
- **E3 — Classify intended vs accidental.** Tag each downstream effect INTENDED (matches an AC) or ACCIDENTAL. **Each ACCIDENTAL effect becomes a question to confirm with the user BEFORE coding.** INTENDED effects become evidence in the doc.
- **E4 — Author the LLD FIRST** (see below). This is the "design first, then proceed" gate — do NOT open a source file until the LLD's current-behavior + gap + proposed-design sections exist.
- **E5 — Spawn the preliminary `changes/<slug>.md`**, filling only its architecture/design sections as a SHORT summary that links to the LLD. Leave the rest as placeholders. **Do NOT commit the LLD or the change doc yet** — both are working files until wrap-up.

Tiny work (typo, label rename, comment) collapses E1-E5 to a one-sentence note in the doc — but run the step explicitly so the record exists.

### The Low-Level Design (LLD)

The design output of pre-coding analysis. Copy [`docs/_templates/_TEMPLATE_lld.md`](../../docs/_templates/_TEMPLATE_lld.md) to `docs/lld/<work-id>-<short-kebab-slug>.md`. It proves you understand the system before touching it: what it does today, the precise gap, and the minimal proposed change with alternatives weighed and cascading impact mapped.

Sections: header + summary; Purpose & scope; Intake (transcribed AC text); Domain mapping; Current behavior (+ diagram); Gap analysis; Before-state evidence (read-only queries + headline numbers); Proposed design (principle, mechanism, where it plugs in, alternatives w/ pros-cons); Affected components; Cascading impact (intended vs accidental); Draft test scenarios; Open questions & reverse-KT; Pre-implementation checklist; References.

Authoring rules:
- **Read the latest source, don't guess** — pull every component you describe and cite concrete paths/methods/fields.
- **Evidence over assertion** — back the gap with read-only queries and numbers; back behavior with actual code paths.
- **Design minimally** — smallest additive change reusing existing mechanisms; always list rejected alternatives so the next person doesn't re-litigate.
- **Separate decided from pending** — park unconfirmed assumptions in the Open-questions section; never bake an unconfirmed predicate into the design as settled.

The LLD is mandatory for non-trivial work. Two **conditional** companions (do NOT produce by reflex): `docs/lld/<work-id>-questions-and-kt.md` (only when assumptions need stakeholder confirmation; [`_TEMPLATE_questions-and-kt.md`](../../docs/_templates/_TEMPLATE_questions-and-kt.md)) and `docs/lld/<work-id>-walkthrough.md` (only when a live alignment session is warranted; [`_TEMPLATE_walkthrough.md`](../../docs/_templates/_TEMPLATE_walkthrough.md)). The change doc summarizes + links to the LLD — never duplicate the full design into both.

