## UT / UAT test-evidence docs (when asked)

When the user asks for a "UT doc", "UAT doc", "test evidence", "QA walkthrough", or "screenshot proof each AC works": produce a minimal, screenshot-heavy before→after walkthrough that PROVES each AC by running the REAL process end-to-end. Evidence beats prose.

**Shape:** `docs/ut/<work-id>/<slug>.md` + sibling `assets/` (+ optional PDF). One `#` title + ONE intro line; one `##` per scenario (`AC1`, `AC2`, …), each = ONE sentence (condition → expected outcome) + before screenshots + after screenshots with captions naming the concrete IDs/values. NO "Purpose / Components / Reproduce / Summary" sections. Screenshot names predictable: `ac1-before-<what>.png`, `ac1-after-<what>.png`.

**Staging + run protocol:**
1. FIND a real record the process will pick up (prefer real over synthetic). If none qualifies, MODIFY one so it does; for a negative case, FORCE the gap on a real record.
2. **Snapshot everything you modify** (persist original values) so it can be restored.
3. Verify eligibility + blast radius with a query BEFORE running — only your intended records should be processed.
4. Capture ALL before screenshots → run the ACTUAL shipped process ONCE (not the underlying function directly) → verify outcomes with queries (counts, IDs, error codes) → capture ALL after screenshots.
5. **Roll back** the staged/forced changes from the snapshot; LEAVE the process-created records (the evidence) in place.

Virtualized/iframed lists (reports, infinite-scroll grids) usually can't be searched via automation — scroll the container programmatically until the target row renders, then screenshot (newest-first sorts surface a just-created record at the top). Never fake a row that isn't there.

**Commit (only when asked, two-commit, explicit staging):** Commit 1 = staging/rollback scripts + UT doc + screenshots + PDF. Commit 2 = backfill the living/feature doc with links, referencing commit 1's hash.

---

## Anti-patterns (all touchpoints)

- Starting code without a confirmed ticket — extract the number from the message/screenshot, or ASK if it's missing (never guess) — or forgetting to rename the chat to `<ticket> - <description>` after confirming, or treating a screenshot as the source of truth instead of transcribing it.
- Letting "see attached screenshot" land in §3.
- Skipping the pre-coding analysis / writing the LLD AFTER coding as a rationalization. The LLD is a design tool — author it before code.
- Producing the questions-and-kt or walkthrough doc by reflex when there are no open questions; duplicating the full design into both the LLD and the change doc.
- Inlining the doc into the code commit, or skipping the doc "because the commit message says it all" (it has no rollback/verification/follow-ups).
- `git add force-app/` (blanket-add) when another agent may be active; including someone else's file because it was "already modified".
- Missing the `**Manifest:**` line for a `--manifest` deploy; skipping Diff-highlights for OmniScripts/IPs/DRs/layouts; pasting a 500-line file into a diff fence.
- UT: synthetic data when a real record exists; modifying real records without snapshot+rollback; calling the underlying function instead of the shipped process; a report screenshot that doesn't actually show the target row.

## Adapting to other projects

Stack-agnostic. Map terms to your stack: process under test → batch/job/endpoint; record → entity/row; component/engine → class/module/service; downstream automation → triggers/jobs/events; read-only query → DB/API read. Keep `changes/`, `docs/lld/`, `docs/ut/` at the repo root (or adjust paths consistently here and in the templates). Sandbox alias is `{{ORG_ALIAS}}` in this repo; set per-project from `sf config get target-org`.
